\chapter{Closures and lexical capture}
\label{ch:12}
\index{Closures and lexical capture}
\index{Closures!environment ownership}

A lambda can retain immutable free values after the scope that created it ends. For \OnxCodeTerm{add\_by}, the surviving value is the captured increment. For a composition closure, the captures are themselves callables. When another nested lambda needs the same value, capture analysis propagates the dependency through the containing function. Capture order is derived from checked source use.

\OnxInputCode[firstline=2,lastline=15]{../../on1x/examples/closures.on1x}
The C11 path lifts a lambda to static code with a typed environment argument. The environment retains managed captures and releases them when the last owning reference goes away. A function without captures can use a null environment. Capturing mutable locals or JSON owners/views falls outside this profile. An experiment with two closures capturing the same immutable string should keep both valid after their factory returns. A companion negative test should check that replacing the capture with a mutable binding is rejected by the compiler.

\section{Draw the environment reached by a call}
Make two columns, one for code and one for data. The functions made by
calling the addition factory with 1 and with 10 have the same instruction
to execute: add the argument to the saved amount. Their data columns
must differ. If the implementation kept a pointer to a single scratch
slot for the factory parameter, a second factory call could change the
behavior of the first result. Calling the first closure again after the
second has been created should still add one.

Now draw the result of composing the two. Its data column needs a way
to call each of the supplied operations with \emph{its own} data.
For the shipped example the inner call takes 31 to 41 and the outer
call takes 41 to 42. This particular numerical result would also be
obtained in reverse order. To test the composition order, use doubling
as the outer operation and adding one as the inner operation. On input
3 the result must be 8 rather than 7. The doubling function need not
capture anything, but the saved adding function must keep its increment
alive. When the composition environment is released, releasing its field
for the adding function drops one reference; another live copy of the
adding function remains callable.

